## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/contentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackContext`): This is the React context provider that supplies all Contentstack content to the component tree (via useContent → getValue). Live Preview requires the provider to subscribe to the Contentstack Live Preview SDK's onEntryChange events and merge draft content into the context value in real time. Every component that displays CMS content reads from this context, so this is the single point where preview data flow is controlled — the CAUSE, not any individual display component.
  - **Minimal fix:** In the ContentstackContext provider component, initialize the Contentstack Live Preview SDK (e.g., ContentstackLivePreview.init({ stackDetails: { apiKey, deliveryToken, environment }, clientUrl }) and add an onEntryChange callback that merges the incoming draft entry data into the context's content state, triggering a re-render of all useContent consumers. The provider's value must be updated from the live preview callback rather than only from the initial page props. If the Stack SDK is already initialized elsewhere for delivery, add live_preview: { enable: true, host: PREVIEW_HOST } to its config and call livePreviewUpdate in the provider's useEffect.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- With Live Preview active, when an editor changes a text field in the CMS and saves the draft, the new draft text appears on the open preview page.
- The updated draft content appears on the page while it remains open, without the tester manually refreshing the browser or restarting/rebuilding the application.
- When viewing published (non-draft) content, the page displays the expected published values — e.g. the published page heading, body copy, and any CMS-driven labels render with their published text.
- Every visible CMS-driven component on the page — such as the page heading, body content, and banner/hero text — displays the updated draft value after the corresponding field is edited in the CMS.
- When CMS content for a field is unavailable or empty, the page renders without crashing and the affected area shows an empty or fallback state rather than an error or broken layout.
- After a second edit to the same field in the CMS, the page displays the most recently saved draft value, with no stale earlier value remaining visible.
- Draft content shown in Live Preview is confined to the preview session: the published values listed in the published-content check remain what visitors of the non-preview page see.

